Title: The Cross-Roads
Order: 5
Production Code: 1x05

EXT. MISSOURI LANDING - NIGHT

PAGE: 1

PANEL: Torchlight at the edge of the water. Men pour down to the landing as fast as they can come, hats, knives, rifles, faces twisted with drink and excitement. A wagon stands in the center with Warren lying bound in the bed, one shoulder bloodied, gold hair matted with dirt.

CAPTION: The crowds which surged through the streets day and night were rushing toward the wagon where lay the prisoner.

PANEL: THOMSON mounts the wagon-seat, arm out, orating to the mob; he wears a theatrical frown, a bottle in the other hand.

PANEL: GIDEON HOLMES at the tailboard, spreading his arms to the crowd like a man selling something.

HOLMES
Gentlemen, take notice! This yere man is a abolitionist an' a nigger thief, two crimes we never overlooks.

PANEL: Holmes, grinning, hand cupped to his ear.

HOLMES (CONT'D)
What's your will, gentlemen? Speak out.

PANEL: The mob, a wall of shouting faces with Warren small under them.

CROWD
Give him a thrashing first!

CROWD (CONT'D)
Hang him! Burn him!

PAGE: 2

PANEL: The ruffians drag Warren from the wagon and throw themselves on him; boots, fists, spit; his bright head rises and falls in the crowd.

SFX: THUD

PANEL: The crowd packed into a street, a dark river of men.

CROWD
To the cross-roads!

PANEL: Close on Warren: a rope knotted about his neck, half-running, half-dragged, blood on his mouth.

PANEL: He falls; the executioners lift him up, loosen the rope and give him time to breathe, laughing.

PANEL: Warren on his feet, tall, looking at the crowd with scorn.

CAPTION: The British idea of fair play was in his mind.

PAGE: 3

EXT. THE CROSS-ROADS - NIGHT

PANEL: A cross-roads, a tall sign-post, a ring of torches. Men are running up with armloads of wood from every side.

PANEL: Warren dragged to the foot of the sign-post and bound with his arms against his sides, the wounded one twisted; wood piled about him but not as high as his knees.

CAPTION: A slow fire steadily increased would prolong the enjoyment.

PANEL: Thomson coming out of the crowd carrying a blazing pine knot; Warren's eyes meet his; not a word passes.

PANEL: A wee tot in the crowd, felled backwards by the legs around him, goggling up at the stake in the torchlight.

PANEL: Close on Warren's face; the crowd's roar going quiet in his ears; a crown of fire somewhere above him.

CAPTION: There came to him a realizing sense of all the Immortal Son must have suffered on His way to Golgotha.

PAGE: 4

PANEL: The first grey streak of dawn through the trees; the stars fading; cocks crowing very faintly from far-off farms.

PANEL: The ferry crew rushing wood from the boat's store; others heaping fuel on a camp-fire; a man dispensing drinks at a temporary bar in the back of a wagon.

PANEL: A rowdy swaggering about with his cup, howling for the sacrifice; behind him the stake with its festive air of a May-pole.

PANEL: Thomson with a flourish applies the torch to the pile; the crowd gives a deafening cheer and then goes silent.

PANEL: A small crackling flame at the feet of the stake; the whole circle holding its breath to hear the first scream.

PAGE: 5 (SPLASH)

PANEL: Full-page splash: the stake in the first light, flames rising to Warren's knees, his shirt open at the throat, his face white and set in the fierce glare; ring on ring of staring faces behind him, torches guttering in the dawn, rose sky overhead. At the far right edge of the page, a cloud of dust and the dark shapes of galloping riders; a few heads in the crowd have already turned.
LIGHT: Firelight on the front of the figure, rose dawn behind

CAPTION: Warren ground his teeth, determined to die and make no moan to please and gratify the crowd.

SFX: CLOP CLOP CLOP

PAGE: 6

PANEL: Riders thundering in at a gallop, led by COLONEL TITUS in the uniform of a State constable, a dozen men behind him. The crowd falls back respectfully.

COLONEL TITUS
Halt!

PANEL: The Colonel springs from his horse and clears the open space in front of the sign-post in one bound.

PANEL: The Colonel and his men kicking the blazing wood away from the stake, scattering it with hands and feet; the Colonel's coat smoking, his face aflame with effort.

PANEL: The Colonel cuts Warren's bonds with a clasp-knife while the constables stamp out the last embers. The crowd watches in sullen silence.

PANEL: The Colonel turns to the mob.

COLONEL TITUS
Fools! What are you doing?

HOLMES
Burning a nigger-thief.

PAGE: 7

PANEL: The Colonel ignores Holmes and turns on Thomson, who stands with the brand still in his hand.

COLONEL TITUS
None of your monkeyin', Bill Thomson; speak up. You had charge of this affair.

PANEL: The Colonel, white with anger, controlled.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
You have been allowing your men to do that which will put us in the power of every Northern mudsill of an abolitionist.

PANEL: Close on Thomson and the Colonel, level, almost in whispers; the crowd a blur behind them.

THOMSON
Dead men tell no tales.

PANEL: The Colonel with an answering glance, like a man agreeing to a price.

COLONEL TITUS
True. But let all things be done in decency and order and according to the process of law.

PAGE: 8

PANEL: The Colonel facing the crowd, one hand out toward Warren, voice raised.

COLONEL TITUS
This man is a British subject.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
How are we to impress the world with our fair and impartial dealing,

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
if you go to work and call us liars by breaking the slate?

PANEL: Murmurs of approval in the crowd; a man calls out.

JIM MURPHY
Fac' is thar's nothin' fer us to do but to light out, ain't that the idee, Colonel?

PANEL: The crowd dispersing slowly, men looking at their boots.

COLONEL TITUS
That's the idea, Murphy; burn the wind the whole caboodle of you!

PANEL: Thomson steps forward with a swagger and a sneer.

THOMSON
'Pears to me you're d----d finicky 'bout law an' all that jes' this particular time.

PANEL: Thomson, grinning, leaning on the sign-post.

THOMSON (CONT'D)
S'pose the law lets the prisoner go free? You'll be a real pop'lar candidate fer Missouri's next gov'ner.

PAGE: 9

PANEL: The Colonel's face changes: an ugly, brutal look that sends a shudder down Warren's back, held up by the constables.

COLONEL TITUS
No fear of that in this State.

PANEL: The Colonel snaps at the constables.

COLONEL TITUS (CONT'D)
Gentlemen, secure the prisoner. Thomson, fall in there and lend a hand; be quick about it.

PANEL: Thomson gives him a long look and without a word mounts his horse and rides away. Not with the troop.

PANEL: Warren lifted to the floor of a wagon, constables on each side; the Colonel standing back with his hands behind him.

PANEL: The wagon driven rapidly down a road toward a town; the smoking remains of the stake receding behind it.

EXT. BROWN'S CAMP - DAY

PAGE: 10

PANEL: The camp at noon: fugitives at work, a woman hoeing, a man splitting wood with a musket against the stump; children at their lessons under a tree; and in the center of it John Brown with an open Bible, reading aloud to a ring of listeners.

CAPTION: The fugitive slaves who came in fear and trembling were strengthened by contact with the free, strong spirit of their rescuer.

PANEL: BROWN, spare, upright, a Puritan with a gentle eye, reads to the children.

BROWN
He hath made of one blood all the nations of the earth.

PANEL: The same scene, a child repeating the verse, Brown nodding.

BROWN (CONT'D)
Remember those in bonds as bound with them.

PANEL: Winona quartered in the Brown family's hut; Brown comes in and rests a rugged hand on her short-cropped curls; she looks up at him.

BROWN (CONT'D)
My pretty squaw.

PANEL: Winona alone in the woods, climbing to a seat on the high rocks, her hand shading her eyes, looking east, then west.

CAPTION: There was not a day nor an hour that she did not think of Maxwell.

PAGE: 11

PANEL: Winona, finding a hole in under the bluff; she goes down into a tunnel and enters a cavern.

CAPTION: Some impulse of the wild things among whom she had lived drove her to a hole in under the bluff.

PANEL: In the cave she makes a divan of dried moss and lies at full length, staring at the ceiling; light from the tunnel's mouth.

PANEL: Her face, hands cold, eyes open wide, projecting everything into the air.

WINONA
Come back to me!

PANEL: Judah, in the camp, leaving at dawn with a rifle; Brown giving him his orders with a hand on his shoulder.

CAPTION: Judah's capabilities were discovered very soon by Captain Brown and his sons.

PANEL: Judah in the woods on a bed of leaves, rifle across his chest, staring up through the branches; the weight of an unsaid word.

JUDAH
Why should I hesitate? We are of the same condition in life in the eyes of the world.

PAGE: 12

PANEL: Morning. Judah building a fire in a deep ravine and scattering the embers that the smoke should give no sign; waiting near her favorite haunts.

PANEL: Winona coming down the path, very pale, her face bearing traces of mental suffering; Judah behind a tree watching her with hungry eyes.

PANEL: The low cry breaks from him; the next instant the girl faces him. Her hands shut, her eyes dry and hard, knowing what he suffers.

JUDAH
Winona!

PANEL: Winona sits trembling on the rocks, arms folded on her knees, forehead down.

WINONA
Oh, Judah! Haven't we been through enough without this?

PANEL: Judah standing close, helpless; hesitating.

JUDAH
I'm going away, Winona, as soon as you are safe in Canada.

JUDAH (CONT'D)
I want you to know I've been sorrowing over your sorrow,

JUDAH (CONT'D)
and hoping you might think of me as the one who would die for you.

PAGE: 13

PANEL: Winona shakes her head, tears falling to the ground.

WINONA
I am sorry. So very sorry. But you see, Judah, it cannot be; I have no love to give.

PANEL: Judah bursts out; his hand slams a tree.

JUDAH
The white man gets it all. All!

PANEL: Winona, firmly, rising to face him.

WINONA
Do you forget all that Mr. Maxwell has done for us? He knows not of my love and will never know.

WINONA (CONT'D)
Is he to blame?

PANEL: The two of them walking toward the camp in the twilight, trees tossing their arms, stars coming out; only the tears of the girl answering him.

JUDAH
What moral responsibility rests upon him from whom all good things are taken? Answer me that.

PANEL: A month later: wild flowers in every stream, wild grapes in the air; at the edge of the camp a dishevelled woman with two children, barefoot, running.

CAPTION: A month had passed since the fugitives had reached the camp.

PAGE: 14 (TURN)

PANEL: Parson Steward's wife, wild and dishevelled, with her two children clinging to her skirt, in the middle of the camp, the women round her.

MRS. STEWARD
My heavenly marster! The Rangers done caught my husband and shot him; they've carried the young Englishman to jail.

PANEL: Brown draws himself up like a prophet, lightning in his mild eyes.

CAPTION: No one slept that night when the fate of the two gallant men was known.

PANEL: A council at the fire, Brown with a hand on a map, Maybee and a dozen armed men. Maybee volunteers with a hand up.

PANEL: Winona in the woods leaning against a tree with her eyes shut and her fists tight.

WINONA
Help me to help save him!

PANEL: Morning. Winona at the door of Brown's hut, hand raised to knock. Her face is quite calm.

CAPTION: In the morning she sought an interview with Captain Brown.

INT. THE MISSOURI JAIL - DAY

PAGE: 15

PANEL: A bare room in a frame building in the heart of a Missouri city: plank walls, one barred window, a pale grey light. Warren is pushed in through the door by constables with a rifle butt; a crowd of men fills the passage.

CAPTION: Warren was pushed into a bare room and told that it was his quarters until business hours.

PANEL: A man with a candle seizes Warren's sound arm and looks him over from head to foot, insultingly.

PANEL: Warren shakes him off, furious.

WARREN
Do you call yourself a man to so insult a wounded stranger?

PANEL: The man, spitting.

JAILER'S MAN
Don't you dare speak to a white man except to answer questions, you d----d nigger-thief!

PANEL: Warren, desperate and proud.

WARREN
I shall appeal to the British consul for protection from your vile insults. It will cost your government dear for to-night's business.

PAGE: 16

INT. THE COURT HOUSE - DAY

PANEL: A large unfinished room with bare brick walls and open rafters, packed with the unwashed Democracy of Missouri; pipes, spit, hats. From the rafters directly above the dock hang three new ropes, each with the hangman's knot.

CAPTION: The court proceeded with its farcical mockery of justice.

PANEL: Warren, dishevelled, hand-cuffed, standing in the dock, scribbling on a sheet of paper.

PANEL: A magistrate with a heavy face, to whom Warren hands the paper; the magistrate throws it aside without reading.

WARREN
I, the undersigned, a British subject, do hereby protest against every step taken thus far by the State of Missouri in this case.

PANEL: Colonel Titus on the witness stand, hand raised, speaking gravely. Thomson on the bench behind him with his arms folded.

COLONEL TITUS
The young man basely betrayed my hospitality by aiding my slaves, Winona and Judah, to escape.

PANEL: The jury, twelve men, saying "guilty" without leaving their seats.

PAGE: 17

PANEL: The judge, rigid and pious, holds up a hand.

JUDGE
Warren Maxwell, you have been guilty of aiding slaves to run away and depart from their master's service. For it you are to die!

PANEL: The judge, quoting, a finger on the page.

JUDGE (CONT'D)
You are young; quite too young to be where you are.

JUDGE (CONT'D)
If you had remembered your Creator in your past days, you would not now be in a felon's place.

PANEL: The judge reads the sentence, the ropes swaying above Warren's head.

JUDGE (CONT'D)
You be taken to the State prison for one year; and there be closely confined until Friday, the 26th day of May next.

PANEL: The judge, harder.

JUDGE (CONT'D)
On which day you will be taken to the place of public execution,

JUDGE (CONT'D)
and there be hanged by the neck till your body be dead.

JUDGE (CONT'D)
May God have mercy on your soul.

PANEL: Warren in the dock, a stupor; the words carrying no meaning to him.

PAGE: 18

INT. THE STATE PRISON, WARREN'S CELL - DAY

PANEL: A room sixteen feet square with a small grated window at each end, a cot, a broom, a hole in the floor where a stove-pipe used to go. Warren sits on the cot with his arm in a sling, burning with fever.

CAPTION: Under the room was another of the same size, used as a lock-up for slaves.

PANEL: Through the stove-hole: a view into the crowded lower room, bodies and faces and hands in grey light, a man holding up papers.

CAPTION: One man who had his free papers on his person produced them to prove the truth of his story.

PANEL: The official in the lower room, lighting the papers in a candle flame; the man's face blank with horror.

CAPTION: The official took the papers from him, burned them, and sold him the next week at public auction.

PANEL: Warren at the stove-hole, sees something below, and falls fainting backward on the floor; an arm over his face.

CAPTION: He had seen what must not be described in the interests of decency and humanity.

PANEL: Night: a candle, two physicians and a slave in the doorway; Warren on the cot tossing, wild-eyed, hands in the air.

PAGE: 19

PANEL: Warren's delirium, a collage: the flames at the stake, the shot that killed Steward, the rush of water as he lowered Winona from the Crescent, a black pit with voices in it.

CAPTION: To the sick man the wearing, jarring sound of voices rising out of a black pit was ever present and unbearable.

PANEL: Close on his face, tossing, a cloth on his brow; a pair of slim dark hands wringing it out.

PANEL: A young nurse, ALLEN PINKS, a mulatto boy with closely cropped hair like velvet, delicate brown features, large dark eyes, bending over the cot; the face turned away from us. [[ART NOTE: this is Winona in disguise. Never give a clear full-face view of Allen until Issue 6.]]

CAPTION: The nurse was a young mulatto known as Allen Pinks.

PANEL: Allen at the stove-hole, giving orders to the men below in a low tone of authority.

ALLEN
Hush your noise! Haven't I told you he must sleep?

PANEL: A rough voice from the hole, a low laugh, a flash of teeth.

PRISONER (O.P.)
Got a black boss dis time.

PAGE: 20

PANEL: Allen back at the cot: a light touch on Warren's hair; one tear falls on his cheek. The nurse has kissed the patient.

PANEL: The door closes behind the lad. Warren's eyes open in full consciousness; he brushes the tear from his cheek with a puzzled look.

PANEL: Allen returns with a bowl of soup; his features light up on finding Warren awake.

ALLEN (CONT'D)
Well, how are you getting on, sir?

PANEL: Warren, weak, reaching out a hand.

WARREN
Who are you? Haven't I met you somewhere? Your voice has a familiar sound.

ALLEN
I fancy you don't know me.

PANEL: Allen, holding the spoon, a significant gaze.

ALLEN (CONT'D)
If you will only remain quiet and easy in your mind, there's no doubt all may yet be well.

PAGE: 21

PANEL: A week later. Warren sitting up on the cot with the broth; Allen on a stool, feeling his pulse; summer heat in the room.

CAPTION: Maxwell was fascinated by his nurse.

PANEL: Close on Allen's face seen from behind: the velvet cropped hair, the carriage of the head. Warren, watching him, frowning with half a memory.

CAPTION: He could not shake off the idea that somewhere he had known the lad before in his life.

PANEL: Allen, matter-of-fact.

ALLEN
Mr. Maxwell, I'm not to tend you any longer after this week. I'm to be sold.

ALLEN (CONT'D)
It's getting too hot for me, and I'm going to run for it.

PANEL: Allen leans in close, whispering, one eye on the door.

ALLEN (CONT'D)
Have you no hope of escape? Have you never thought of being rescued?

PANEL: Warren, in speechless joy.

WARREN
Oh, Allen!

PAGE: 22 (TURN)

PANEL: Allen glances along the corridor to see that no one is listening.

ALLEN
I am here in the interest of your friends! I leave to-night.

ALLEN (CONT'D)
We must hasten our plans, for Thomson is expected on a visit here any day.

PANEL: Warren shudders, a hand to his throat.

WARREN
I fear that man.

PANEL: Allen, steady, standing at the door.

ALLEN
Keep up your heart. Barring accident, you will soon be free.

PANEL: Night. Allen walks across the yard with a wooden bucket toward the well.

PANEL: The well in the dark, a bucket on the stones; the yard gate open. No one in sight.

PANEL: Morning. The jailer OWENS, shouting in the yard with a lantern, men running with torches; the bucket at the well, empty, a boy's shoe beside it.

CAPTION: The alarm was given, but no trace of the boy was found.
